WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.918
This is Tale — the workspace
where your team puts AI to work.

2
00:00:04.918 --> 00:00:09.916
Over the next three minutes, you will see
how it fits together — and what it takes

3
00:00:09.916 --> 00:00:10.830
to use AI well.

4
00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.920
This is your workspace.

5
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.771
Chat with AI models,
projects for your team,

6
00:00:16.771 --> 00:00:22.214
agents, automations, and the knowledge
your company runs on — everything one sidebar

7
00:00:22.214 --> 00:00:22.927
click away.

8
00:00:22.927 --> 00:00:27.270
Let us start where your team will
spend most of its time: the chat.

9
00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.246
You can ask a model anything.

10
00:00:30.246 --> 00:00:33.002
The real power is
asking about your own work.

11
00:00:33.002 --> 00:00:37.840
We will attach a company document as
context… and ask what customers said about

12
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.310
onboarding last quarter.

13
00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:47.847
Tale shows the model’s reasoning first,
then streams the answer — grounded in your

14
00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.869
documents, with its sources named.

15
00:00:49.869 --> 00:00:52.130
Not a guess you have to take on faith.

16
00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:57.071
Because here is the honest
truth about language models:

17
00:00:57.071 --> 00:01:00.476
without your context, they
answer anyway — fluently,

18
00:01:00.476 --> 00:01:02.637
confidently, and sometimes wrong.

19
00:01:02.637 --> 00:01:04.209
That is a hallucination.

20
00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:09.513
Grounding every answer in your own
documents turns AI from a smooth talker into a

21
00:01:09.513 --> 00:01:11.150
colleague you can verify.

22
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:13.891
That context lives here.

23
00:01:13.891 --> 00:01:17.037
Knowledge holds your
documents, crawled websites,

24
00:01:17.037 --> 00:01:19.926
and structured records
— indexed, searchable,

25
00:01:19.926 --> 00:01:22.750
and citable by every
agent in the workspace.

26
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.380
An agent is AI with a job
description: instructions,

27
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.348
a model, tools, and the
knowledge it may use.

28
00:01:30.348 --> 00:01:33.910
Build it once, and your
whole team can put it to work.

29
00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:40.619
Automations handle the work nobody
should do by hand: triage, drafting, routing.

30
00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:45.990
They run on triggers and schedules, and
every run leaves a full execution log.

31
00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:52.076
And when an automation wants to
act on the world — send the email,

32
00:01:52.076 --> 00:01:55.945
change the record — it can be
required to ask a person first.

33
00:01:55.945 --> 00:01:57.531
AI drafts, people decide.

34
00:01:57.531 --> 00:02:01.970
That one principle prevents most of
what can go wrong with automation.

35
00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.243
Projects bring the team and
the AI to the same table:

36
00:02:07.243 --> 00:02:12.398
shared files, tasks, and discussions,
with agents working alongside your people —

37
00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:13.670
not instead of them.

38
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.614
And you stay in control
of the machinery itself.

39
00:02:18.614 --> 00:02:21.721
You choose which AI
providers run your workloads,

40
00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:25.970
pin your data to a region, and
audit every action in the workspace.

41
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.332
That is Tale: answers
grounded in your truth,

42
00:02:30.332 --> 00:02:34.197
agents with a mandate,
automation with human oversight.

43
00:02:34.197 --> 00:02:38.273
In the next episode, we go
deeper into chat — attachments,

44
00:02:38.273 --> 00:02:40.170
model choice, and research.

45
00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.679
Everything you saw is in the
documentation, alongside this series.

46
00:02:45.679 --> 00:02:47.180
See you in episode two.

